[Music]  hey Google let's talk to angular  firebase.com sure here's the test  version of angular firebase.com howdy  partner i'm angular firebase what can I  do you for what's the latest episode the  last video was episode 104 angular CLI  basics tutorial its description goes  like this master the basics of the  angular v6 CLI the google assistant  makes it easy for users to get things  done in your app through conversation in  today's episode we'll build a Google  assistant app from scratch using  dialogue flow and firebase cloud  functions if you're new here make sure  to LIKE and subscribe and you can grab  the source code at angular firebase comm  let's get started by breaking down the  demo that I just showed you the first  thing that I said was hey Google let's  talk to my app name the app name is  something that you register as the  developer and it signifies the main  entry point into your assistant app when  Google finds this app it's going to  start a session and dialogue flow we've  used dialogue flow before in episode 58  to build a chat bot and it's basically  just a high-level tool for building  conversational experiences it uses some  crazy machine learning algorithms to  handle the natural language processing  so when I say get the latest episode it  knows that I'm trying to convey this get  latest episode intent which in turn is  fulfilled by a firebase cloud function  that goes out and visits the website  gets the information that we need and  then tells the assistant what to say to  the end user and you can even use a rich  card as the response so the user can  just link directly back to your  progressive web app that they already  have installed on their device now that  we know how the basic process works  let's start building our Google  assistant app there are several  different ways we can do this but let's  start in the Google Actions console you  can link your assistant to an existing  firebase or a Google cloud project or  you can just create a new one from  scratch from there you'll select a  category and then the first thing you'll  do is determine the entry point for this  app this parts pretty important because  it's what the user will say to invoke  your app for example hey Google let's  talk to angular firebase com  at this point you could continue  building your app  the actions console and there's a few  templates for doing so but in most cases  you want to switch over to dialogue flow  at this point and start building the  conversation interface click on actions  then add your first action and then  click build go ahead and create the  agent and then switch over to the intent  screen you'll have a default welcome  intent which will greet your user when  they first initiate your app you can  customize the response as needed  but we're going to create a brand new  intent this one's called learn about  angular firebase there are two basic  ways to handle intense the easiest way  is just to create a text response here  in dialog flow which we'll do for this  intent or you can invoke a firebase  cloud function to reach out to the  outside world to retrieve dynamic data  but first we need to get the bot some  training phrases so it knows how to  detect the intent for example what is  angular firebase dialogue flow will use  these phrases to refine the machine  learning algorithm that's used to detect  similar phrases that are said by the  end-user the only other thing you need  at this point is to send the response  back to the user which we can do with  just plain text so when this intent is  triggered it's going to respond to the  user with whatever you put in this text  response and you can add multiple  responses to to give some variation to  your bot so that's the most basic use  case but we want to do something a  little more advanced we want to use a  cloud function to go out to the angular  firebase website and figure out what the  last episode was for that will create a  new intent and then we'll add the  training phrases just like we did before  but this time instead of sending a text  response we're going to flip the switch  for webhook fulfillment this tells  dialogue flow to send some data to our  back-end cloud function then we can use  the dialogue flow SDK for nodejs to  handle these intents and respond back to  the user this makes it possible for your  bot to do things like updated database  reach out to third party API is send  email or anything else you might do in a  node.js back-end you can code your web  hook directly in the browser if you want  to but personally I wouldn't recommend  doing that a better way to do it is to  open up your favorite IDE and then from  the command line run firebase and knit  functions then I highly recommend  using the typescript option and then for  everything else you can just go through  with the main defaults then CD into the  functions directory and install the  actions on Google SDK this will help us  format the responses that go back to  dialog flow so we can build this app  with minimal code then for this project  I'm going to install two other  dependencies that we'll use to build a  simple web scraper which I'll explain in  more detail later for right now just  install node fetch and cheerio then  we'll write all of the code in the index  ts file first we'll import our  dependencies then we can import dialogue  flow from the actions on Google SDK it  also includes a bunch of helpers for  formatting rich cards that you send in  the response for example that card that  would link us back to our progressive  web app then the app itself is very  similar conceptually to Express j/s you  can think of each intent like an HTTP  endpoint that we need to handle whenever  it gets triggered we can say app intent  with get latest episode and then this  will take a callback function to handle  the intent and because we're fetching  data from the outside world  asynchronously will make this an async  function for right now I'm setting up a  placeholder variable called data which  will eventually scrape the web for the  data that we want on angular firebase  comm eventually it will return a title  description and episode number for the  bot to use we can then say conversation  closed or conversation asked to continue  the conversation with the user if you  use ask it's going to keep the  conversation open if you use closed it  will end the dialogue flow session after  that response is sent in this case our  body has nothing else to do so we're  going to use conversation close you  could just pass in a string here but I  think it's easier to use a simple  response which allows you to control  both the text and the speech that the  bot will return so the text will be nice  and concise and then the speech will  contain additional information such as  the full episode description so that's  great for text and speech only  applications but things like smartphones  can handle more than just text and  speech in this case we can send back a  card that will D point to our app and  take the user exactly where they want to  go this works great for both progressive  web apps and native mobile apps  and it's just a great way to build  engagement with your users and that's  pretty much all there is to it on the  dialog flow side the only missing piece  at this point is how do we get this  actual information from the website I'm  not going to talk about this code in  detail because it has nothing to do with  the assistant but all it's doing is  fetching the webpage and then using  cheerio to traverse the DOM to find the  information that we need it's just a  classic web scraper first we need to  fetch the page asynchronously then on  this particular web page we have a feed  of lessons so we want to pull the first  lesson from the page along with its  content then we can extract the text  that is contained within the various  elements inside this div then we'll  return everything is a plain JavaScript  object so we can use it in method that  we had defined earlier to handle the  intent the final step is to export our  function which we'll name fullfillment  it needs to be an HTTP function and it  will take the dialogue flow app as its  argument now open up another command  line terminal and run firebase deploy  only functions when that's finished you  can go to the firebase console and  verify that it's been deployed in the  functions tab and grab the URL endpoint  then switch back over to the fulfillment  tab and dialogue flow and paste it as  the web hook fulfillment URL and that's  pretty much all there is to it you've  now successfully built your own Google  assistant app go back to the actions on  Google console and test it out you can  also test it out on any device that's  connected to your Gmail account that  means you can start testing it out right  away on your Android or iPhone device or  on your Google home device but to  release it to the public you'll go  through a very similar process to the  Google Play Store for Android apps where  you have an alpha beta and then  production release once it's ready to go  wide overall this technology is super  exciting and there's tons of  opportunities for business to engage  their customers in ways that weren't  possible before the demand for  conversation apps is growing rapidly so  it's something you definitely want to be  aware of and familiar with as a  developer I'm gonna go ahead and wrap  things up there if this video helped you  please like and subscribe and if you  have any questions reach out to me on  slack and if you're ready to go beyond  the basics consider becoming a pro  member at angular firebase com to get  access to exclusive content  designed to help you build and ship your  app faster thanks for watching and I'll  see you soon

